[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one. Each problem now has its
own ticket.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix and closing #4471 would leave the other two problems with no ticket.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first.** A day-first tenant books
7 April and the invite says 4 July. The reports that name two dates name a day and a month
swapped. Most of #4471 is this.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints an `MM/DD/YYYY` hint to day-first tenants.** Their people type what
the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The dates come
out swapped too, so it looks like `parser`, but the parser fix does not touch it: the form and its
hint need their own fix. It shows only through the form channel at tenants that run booking-v3.

**`move`: the invite moves by one day.** No month is flipped: the 12 May invite shows 11 May,
a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday while one in Toronto gets Monday, one invite arrived before the
booking was made. That points at time zones, not at date order. The desk had already read these
as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`), but the cause table on #4471 counts them under `parser`.

## How the reports were placed

- One id is one report. The ticket export comes in overlapping pages, so some rows appear twice;
  the counts above drop the repeats.
- The cause comes from the tenant, its locale and its booking form (`#lib/kb/causes`), not from
  the intake tag. The tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and disagrees with the tenant
  often.
- Counts are the desk's own: the cause table's, on the export as it is. The reports the desk read
  as a one-day move stay counted under the cause they are filed under, and get the `move` row
  and ticket of their own (`#lib/kb/problems`).
- A few reports name no dates at all. They stay with their tenant's cause.

## Why they were merged

All three came in under the same subject lines, and [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merges on subject alone. The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) says "when you are unsure, merge".
Here that hid two problems inside a ticket marked known. A new decision record could replace
0001 with a rule that also checks the cause; this page does not change it.

